    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d44">

                <pb facs="0140" n="630" sameAs="frus:frus1948v01p2/pg630"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> S/S–NSC Files: Lot 63D351: NSC 30 Series</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State for
                        Public Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Allen</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d44-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-09-14">September 14, 1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>From the point of view of psychological warfare, I concur fully in the views of
                    EUR and the NSC/30 paper. I might add that public opinion in the United States
                    may have an important bearing on the question and might force the use of atomic
                    weapons, even if the chief executive were inclined against it. The public would
                    refuse to accept American casualties which might be saved by shortening the
                    war.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geo. Allen</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
